{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778375977341,"sessionID":"ses_1f0882a6cffeKbrAF1V15ZuI6R","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_URtgYc3p0BhVIuOEIgjwDXHI","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\n2:   \"body\": \"related #2097\\n\\n\\nPlease check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nquiescent fibroblast\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\ninactive fibroblast - PMID: [22529592](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22529592/)\\n\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\n\\nA fibroblast in a quiescent state, characterized by a smaller, spindle-shaped morphology with a relatively small cytoplasm, modest rough endoplasmic reticulum and condensed chromatin.  Despite low proliferation and contractility, it maintains high metabolic activity for extracellular-matrix homeostasis through continuous matrix protein turnover and mechanosensitive signaling. This cell can rapidly differentiate into contractile myofibroblasts under injury or inflammatory cues to drive tissue repair.\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41427-020-0226-7\\nPMID:35701396\\nPMID: [21049082](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21049082/)\\nPMID: [40538750](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40538750/)\\nWikipedia:Fibroblast\\n\\n**Comments section**\\nHistorically, quiescent fibroblasts in uninjured tissues were often called “fibrocytes,” but over time this distinction faded and the term “fibroblast” came to be used universally, regardless of activation state. Fibrocyte has been repurposed to describe a distinct population of bone marrow–derived, circulating cells that home to sites of injury and contribute to tissue repair and fibrosis (PMID: 35701396).\\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\nfibroblast\\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\n\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\n\\n\\n\",\n3:   \"comments\": [],\n4:   \"title\": \"[NTR] quiescent fibroblast\",\n5:   \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3252\"\n6: }\n\n(End of file - total 6 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\n  \"body\": \"related #2097\\n\\n\\nPlease check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nquiescent fibroblast\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\ninactive fibroblast - PMID: [22529592](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22529592/)\\n\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\n\\nA fibroblast in a quiescent state, characterized by a smaller, spindle-shaped morphology with a relatively small cytoplasm, modest rough endoplasmic reticulum and condensed chromatin.  Despite low proliferation and contractility, it maintains high metabolic activity for extracellular-matrix homeostasis through continuous matrix protein turnover and mechanosensitive signaling. This cell can rapidly differentiate into contractile myofibroblasts under injury or inflammatory cues to drive tissue repair.\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41427-020-0226-7\\nPMID:35701396\\nPMID: [21049082](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21049082/)\\nPMID: [40538750](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40538750/)\\nWikipedia:Fibroblast\\n\\n**Comments section**\\nHistorically, quiescent fibroblasts in uninjured tissues were often called “fibrocytes,” but over time this distinction faded and the term “fibroblast” came to be used universally, regardless of activation state. Fibrocyte has been repurposed to describe a distinct population of bone marrow–derived, circulating cells that home to sites of injury and contribute to tissue repair and fibrosis (PMID: 35701396).\\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\nfibroblast\\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\n\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\n\\n\\n\",\n  \"comments\": [],\n  \"title\": \"[NTR] quiescent fibroblast\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3252\"\n}","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"__issue_context__.json","time":{"start":1778375977266,"end":1778375977336}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0cda8e0a22f2dce50169ffdd2927548190b7607be2da147819"}},"id":"prt_e0f77e8e8001jOi2DC85Fvj5X1","sessionID":"ses_1f0882a6cffeKbrAF1V15ZuI6R","messageID":"msg_e0f77d64e001m6XBCbWLT1KZnt"}}
